---
import ToolLayout from "@site/layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { referencesFor } from "@site/lib/references.ts";
import references from "../references.json";
import Caveat from "@site/components/Caveat.astro";
import { EXERCISES, EQUIPMENT, MUSCLES, isAssumed, type Exercise, type Muscle } from "../web/catalogue.ts";
const { cite } = referencesFor(references as Parameters<typeof referencesFor>[0]);
const muscle = Object.fromEntries(MUSCLES.map((m) => [m.id, m.label]));
const equip = Object.fromEntries(EQUIPMENT.map((e) => [e.id, e.label]));
const needs = (n: string[][]) => n.length ? n.map((g) => g.map((e) => equip[e]).join(" or ")).join("; plus ") : "nothing";
const strength = EXERCISES.filter((e) => e.direct.length);
const cell = (e: Exercise, ms: Muscle[]) => ms.map((m) => muscle[m] + (isAssumed(e, m) ? " ◇" : "")).join(", ") || "–";
const sourced = (e: Exercise) => Object.entries(e.sources ?? {}).map(([m, n]) => `${muscle[m]}: "${n}"`).join("; ");
const aerobic = EXERCISES.filter((e) => e.met?.length);
---
<ToolLayout slug="exercise-planner" page="methods">
  <h1>Methods and sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">How the exercise planner works, where its numbers come from, what has been checked, and what it can't tell you.</p>

  <h2>How suggestions are found</h2>
  <p>
    You choose targets (any of 13 muscle groups, and heart and lungs) and the equipment you have. The planner keeps
    the exercises your equipment allows and that help at least one target, then tries every combination of up to the
    number of exercises you allow. It ranks them by:
  </p>
  <ol class="prose">
    <li>how many targets are covered (a target muscle is covered when one set of each exercise in the list gives it
      at least 1 set, counted as below; heart and lungs is covered by any aerobic activity);</li>
    <li>then the fewest exercises;</li>
    <li>then the most work for the target muscles, up to twice the covered level;</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Exercises that do exactly the same for your targets (for example the bench press and the chest press machine, if
    your only targets are chest and triceps) are searched as one, then swapped for each other to make varied lists.
    The planner picks the best few lists that differ enough from each other ("All different" shares no exercises; "Some
    shared" lets a list share up to half its exercises with an earlier one), preferring, among equals, the more intense
    aerobic activity and the exercises that work more muscles overall. If there aren't enough lists that differ that
    much, the limit is loosened a step at a time, only lists as good as the best are used, and the page says how much
    the later suggestions share. A "covered" level above one set (2 or 3) is reached by listing different exercises
    for the same muscle, never the same exercise twice. If more than 30 exercises could help, it keeps
    the best three for each target plus those that help most overall, and the page says so.
  </p>

  <h2>Counting sets: direct and indirect</h2>
  <p>
    A set of an exercise counts as <strong>1 set</strong> for a muscle it trains <em>directly</em> and as
    <strong>½ set</strong> for a muscle it trains <em>indirectly</em>. This is the "fractional" method of Pelland and
    colleagues, whose meta-regressions of resistance-training studies classified each set as direct (the measured muscle
    was likely the primary force generator) or indirect (the muscle was likely meaningfully trained but as a helper, or
    synergist), and found that the relative evidence for this "fractional" counting was the strongest of the methods they
    compared, for both muscle growth and strength <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />.
  </p>
  <p>
    An earlier review by Schoenfeld and colleagues found that multi-joint exercises such as presses, rows and squats
    can't be assumed to train every muscle involved as much as an exercise aimed at that muscle; for example, during
    squats and the leg press the hamstrings are much less active than the quadriceps. At the time, with little direct
    evidence, they advised counting sets one-for-one and adjusting by judgement <Fragment set:html={cite("schoenfeld-2019")} />.
    The planner follows the later, larger analysis and counts half.
  </p>

  <h2 id="which-muscles">Which muscles each exercise works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>From the study.</strong> Pelland and colleagues list how they classified the exercises in the studies they
    analysed, for these muscles only: the quadriceps, the rectus femoris (one of the four quadriceps muscles), the
    hamstrings, the chest, the front of the shoulders (anterior deltoid), the trapezius, the triceps and the biceps
    (Table 1 of the published article; Table 1A of the archived preprint) <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />.
    Where that table classifies an exercise for one of those muscles, the planner follows it exactly. For example, the
    bench press trains the chest directly and the front shoulders and triceps indirectly; the lat pulldown and seated row
    train the biceps and trapezius indirectly; squats, lunges and the leg press don't count for the hamstrings. The
    authors note that their classification "was not wholly objective".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The planner's own classification.</strong> Every other assignment is the planner's own, made from anatomy.
    That includes all of the lats, glutes, lower back, abs, calves and forearms, which the table doesn't cover, and the
    covered muscles in exercises it doesn't list. Each exercise is described by the joint movements it loads (for
    example, knee and hip extension in a squat), and the muscles that produce those movements come from an open anatomy
    textbook <Fragment set:html={cite("openstax-ap2e")} />: for example the gluteus maximus extends the hip, the hamstrings
    bend the knee and extend the hip, the erector spinae is the main extensor of the spine, the latissimus dorsi pulls the
    upper arm down and back, and the gastrocnemius and soleus point the foot. A muscle producing the exercise's main
    movement counts as direct; one producing a secondary movement counts as indirect.
  </p>
  <Caveat kind="assumption">These assignments are marked ◇ in the planner and in the table below. They are a
    judgement made openly, not a measurement, and the counting method itself was only tested on the muscles and the kind
    of exercises in the study. Studies that measured muscle growth directly will replace them where they exist.</Caveat>
  <p>
    Muscles that only hold the body still (such as the trunk muscles in a squat) are listed as "steadied" and not counted,
    except in exercises whose whole purpose is the hold, such as the plank or the farmer's carry. Counting those is also
    the planner's assumption: the analysis only included exercises that move through a range (lifting and lowering).
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>All {strength.length} exercises and their muscles</summary>
    <div class="details-body table-scroll">
      <p class="small">◇ = the planner's own classification (an assumption). The last column gives the exercise name exactly as
        printed in the study's table, for each muscle taken from it.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Exercise</th><th>Needs</th><th>Direct (1 set)</th><th>Indirect (½ set)</th><th>From Pelland et al., Table 1A</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {strength.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.name}<br /><span class="muted small">{e.moves}</span></td>
              <td class="small">{needs(e.needs)}</td>
              <td>{cell(e, e.direct)}</td>
              <td>{cell(e, e.indirect)}</td>
              <td class="small">{sourced(e) || "None: all assumptions ◇"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Heart and lungs</h2>
  <p>
    Aerobic activities are rated by their MET value (metabolic equivalent: how many times the energy used sitting at rest),
    copied unchanged, with activity codes, from the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities, which compiles measured
    energy costs for adults aged 19 to 59 <Fragment set:html={cite("compendium-2024")} />. The WHO defines moderate intensity
    as 3 to under 6 METs and vigorous intensity as 6 METs or more, and recommends that adults do 150–300 minutes of
    moderate or 75–150 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity a week (or a mix), plus muscle-strengthening activities
    involving all major muscle groups on 2 or more days a week <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2020")} />.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>All {aerobic.length} aerobic activities and their Compendium values</summary>
    <div class="details-body table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Code</th><th class="num">MET</th><th>Compendium description</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {aerobic.flatMap((e) => e.met!.map((r, i) => (
            <tr><td>{i === 0 ? e.name : ""}</td><td>{r.code}</td><td class="num">{r.met.toFixed(1)}</td><td class="small">{r.label}</td></tr>
          )))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Charts</h2>
  <p>
    The charts show fractional sets per muscle from one set of each exercise in a suggestion, on a linear scale that
    grows to fit the largest value and never cuts values off. The navy line marks the covered level for your target muscles,
    and every value is written next to its point, with ✓ or "short", so colour is never the only signal. With fewer than
    three target muscles a bar chart is used, as a radar chart needs at least three axes.
  </p>

  <h2>What has been checked</h2>
  <p>
    Checked line by line against the archived copies: every MET value, code and description; the fractional counting
    method and every classification taken from Table 1A; the WHO recommendations and intensity definitions; and the
    muscle actions used from the anatomy textbook. Details, with dates, are in the repository's
    <code>tools/exercise-planner/VERIFICATION.md</code>.
  </p>
  <Caveat kind="unverified">The planner's own classifications (◇) haven't yet been compared with studies of the
    exercises themselves. Studies that measured muscle growth after training (not just muscle activity during it) are the
    next thing to compare against.</Caveat>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li>The suggestions are lists of exercises, not a training programme: they don't say how heavy, how many repetitions,
      how many sets or how often. Do any new exercise with good technique, starting light.</li>
    <li>"Quadriceps" combines four muscles. The study's table classifies squats, lunges and the leg press as direct for
      three of them but only indirect for the rectus femoris <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />; the planner
      counts them as direct for the group.</li>
    <li>Thirteen muscle groups is a simplification. Within a group, different exercises stress different parts (for
      example, the upper and lower chest).</li>
    <li>Direct and indirect are two broad categories. Real exercises sit on a continuum, and technique, grip and range of
      motion change which muscles work hardest.</li>
    <li>The analysis behind the counting method pooled studies lasting about 10 weeks on average, in healthy participants
      with an average age of about 25 and none over 70, training with lifting and lowering movements
      <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />. It may apply less well to older people, very experienced lifters, or
      holds such as the plank.</li>
    <li>The studies controlled how heavy each set was and how close to failure it went. A set of a bodyweight exercise,
      such as a bodyweight squat, may be much easier than that for a trained person, so counting it as a full set can
      overstate the work.</li>
    <li>MET values are averages for adults aged 19 to 59; your own effort depends on your fitness and pace
      <Fragment set:html={cite("compendium-2024")} />.</li>
    <li>Muscles that only hold the body still aren't counted, although they are under tension and some authors suggest
      counting them as part of a set <Fragment set:html={cite("schoenfeld-2019")} />. The charts may therefore understate
      the work for, say, the lower back in a deadlift.</li>
    <li>"Covered" means "trained by the list at least once", the planner's own threshold for building lists. It is not a
      recommended dose.</li>
  </ul>
</ToolLayout>
